Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "spatial_tme_relational_probe",
"Title": "Does the Foundation Model See the Neighborhood? Testing Whether Pathology Models Encode Spatial-Relational Tumor-Microenvironment Mechanisms, Not Just Local Morphology",
"Short Hypothesis": "The prognostic and biological mechanism of cancer is not local cell identity alone but the spatial-relational organization of the tumor microenvironment (TME): immune infiltration into tumor nests, spatial exclusion of lymphocytes, tumor-stroma boundary geometry. We hypothesize that pathology foundation models trained on large tissue-patch collections internally encode these relational (neighborhood-context) structures, distinct from and beyond their well-documented local cell-type concepts, and that this relational structure (a) is decodable, (b) is causally load-bearing for prognostic prediction, and (c) matches spatial biology as measured by spatial transcriptomics. If instead the models encode only a bag of local morphologies, patch-context ablations and spatial-relation probes will show no structure beyond local cell counts.",
"Related Work": "A wave of pathology interpretability work (PICASSO/Kim et al. 2026; CytoSAE 2025; Mammo-SAE 2025; 'Learning biologically relevant features via SAEs' 2024; ICFL microscopy 2024) uses sparse autoencoders/dictionary learning to recover monosemantic features that match LOCAL morphological annotations — cell types, calcifications, tissue classes. Concept-MIL and ConceptCLIP link patch concepts to diagnosis; representational-similarity and robustness-index papers characterize global embedding geometry and center confounds. Critically, all validate features against MARGINAL, local annotations (what a patch contains), not against the SPATIAL-RELATIONAL organization among cells/regions that constitutes the actual cancer-biology mechanism (immune infiltration, exclusion, boundary structure) known to drive prognosis. No prior work tests whether pathology FMs encode neighborhood-context relational structure, whether that structure is causally used, or whether it aligns with spatial-transcriptomics ground truth. Our relational, causal, spatial-omics-grounded test is the novel contribution, moving beyond 'the model recognizes cell X' to 'the model represents that immune cells are excluded from the tumor nest.'",
"Abstract": "Interpretability studies of pathology foundation models have shown they encode local morphological concepts such as cell types and tissue classes. But the mechanism that actually governs cancer behavior and prognosis is relational and spatial: whether cytotoxic lymphocytes infiltrate a tumor nest or are excluded from it, the geometry of the tumor-stroma boundary, and immune-desert versus inflamed ecologies. We ask whether pathology foundation models internally represent this spatial-relational tumor-microenvironment (TME) structure, beyond a bag of local morphologies, and whether that structure is causally load-bearing and biologically faithful. Using public whole-slide datasets with paired or co-registered spatial transcriptomics (e.g., 10x Xenium/Visium colorectal and lung cohorts) and immunohistochemistry, we extract patch- and neighborhood-level representations from several public pathology FMs (UNI2, Virchow2, Prov-GigaPath, CONCH). First, we build relational probes that predict TME relationships defined from cell segmentation and spatial statistics — infiltration score, immune-tumor colocalization/exclusion, boundary curvature — and test whether these are decodable beyond local cell-count baselines. Second, we run causal context-ablation experiments: we swap or scramble a patch's spatial neighbors and measure whether relational features and prognostic predictions change as predicted by TME biology (e.g., breaking infiltration should shift an immune-inflamed feature toward immune-desert). Third, we validate against spatial transcriptomics by testing whether relational features align with measured immune-signaling gene programs (T-cell cytotoxicity, IFN-gamma) at the same tissue location. Metrics quantify a relational-beyond-local index, context-ablation causal selectivity, and transcriptomic alignment. Either outcome sharpens what these clinically-deployed models actually 'see.'",
"Experiments": [
"1. Data and ground-truth relations: Assemble public WSI cohorts with (a) cell segmentation/classification (via HoVer-Net or provided annotations) and (b) co-located spatial transcriptomics (10x Xenium/Visium colorectal, lung; or IHC for CD8/CD3/PanCK). Define ground-truth TME relational descriptors per neighborhood window: immune infiltration score (fraction of lymphocytes within tumor nests), immune-tumor spatial exclusion (cross-type Ripley's K / colocalization), tumor-stroma boundary curvature/interface length, and inflamed/excluded/desert phenotype label.",
"2. Representation extraction: For UNI2, Virchow2, Prov-GigaPath, CONCH, extract patch embeddings and, using each model's spatial context (patch grids / slide-aggregator neighborhood attention where available), extract neighborhood-context representations. Train sparse autoencoders on these to obtain candidate relational features, in addition to raw-embedding linear probes.",
"3. Relational-beyond-local test: Train probes predicting each relational descriptor from model representations. Compare against a strict LOCAL baseline that has access only to the same window's cell-type counts/densities (no spatial arrangement). Primary metric: Relational-Beyond-Local Index = probe R^2(model) minus probe R^2(local-count baseline). Positive index for infiltration/exclusion/boundary indicates encoded spatial relations beyond bag-of-cells.",
"4. Neighborhood-permutation control: Spatially scramble cells within a window while preserving cell-type counts, re-render/re-embed (or, for grid models, permute neighbor patches). If model features are truly relational, predictions of infiltration/exclusion should collapse under scrambling while local-morphology features remain stable. Metric: relational-feature sensitivity to scrambling vs. local-feature stability.",
"5. Causal context-ablation: For patch-context or neighborhood-attention models, replace a tumor patch's true immune-rich neighbors with immune-poor neighbors (and vice versa) and measure whether the recovered 'inflamed' relational feature and the downstream prognostic/subtype prediction shift in the biologically-correct direction. Controls: swap with neighbors matched on staining/center but of the same TME phenotype (should not shift). Metric: context-ablation causal selectivity = |shift_relation-breaking| / |shift_matched-control|.",
"6. Spatial-transcriptomics faithfulness: At co-registered locations, test whether recovered relational features correlate with measured immune-signaling gene programs (T-cell cytotoxicity, IFN-gamma response, exhaustion) beyond what local cell-type composition predicts. Metric: partial Spearman correlation controlling for local cell composition; a mechanistic representation should retain signal after this control.",
"7. Prognostic load-bearing test: In cohorts with survival/outcome, quantify how much prognostic performance is attributable to relational features vs. local features via feature-group ablation, testing whether the model's clinically-relevant signal rides on encoded TME relations.",
"8. Cross-model and center-confound checks: Report which models encode relational structure most strongly, and control for medical-center/stain confounds (using the Robustness-Index approach) to ensure relational signal is not a center artifact."
],
"Risk Factors and Limitations": [
"Grid-based FMs process patches independently, so 'context' must come from the slide-level aggregator; models with weak or absent spatial context may show no relational structure by construction — we report per-model and use neighborhood-attention aggregators where available.",
"Re-rendering scrambled tissue for causal ablation is nontrivial; for patch-grid models we operate at the neighbor-patch level (permute/swap embeddings), which is feasible, while pixel-level scrambling is used only where re-embedding is cheap.",
"Co-registration between H&E and spatial transcriptomics is imperfect, adding noise to the transcriptomic-faithfulness test; we use datasets with high-quality registration and report registration-error sensitivity.",
"Local cell counts and spatial relations are correlated in real tissue, making the relational-beyond-local separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with matched controls and partial-correlation analyses but residual confounding may attenuate effects.",
"Center/stain confounds strongly organize pathology embeddings (per the Robustness-Index literature); relational signal could be confounded and we explicitly control for it.",
"A negative result (models encode only local morphology, no relational TME structure) is scientifically decisive and clinically important, though possibly perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as a sharpening of what deployed pathology FMs represent.",
"All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public data, keeping compute at academic scale."
]
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'spatial_tme_relational_probe', 'Title': 'Does the Foundation Model See the Neighborhood? Testing Whether Pathology Models Encode Spatial-Relational Tumor-Microenvironment Mechanisms, Not Just Local Morphology', 'Short Hypothesis': 'The prognostic and biological mechanism of cancer is not local cell identity alone but the spatial-relational organization of the tumor microenvironment (TME): immune infiltration into tumor nests, spatial exclusion of lymphocytes, tumor-stroma boundary geometry. We hypothesize that pathology foundation models trained on large tissue-patch collections internally encode these relational (neighborhood-context) structures, distinct from and beyond their well-documented local cell-type concepts, and that this relational structure (a) is decodable, (b) is causally load-bearing for prognostic prediction, and (c) matches spatial biology as measured by spatial transcriptomics. If instead the models encode only a bag of local morphologies, patch-context ablations and spatial-relation probes will show no structure beyond local cell counts.', 'Related Work': "A wave of pathology interpretability work (PICASSO/Kim et al. 2026; CytoSAE 2025; Mammo-SAE 2025; 'Learning biologically relevant features via SAEs' 2024; ICFL microscopy 2024) uses sparse autoencoders/dictionary learning to recover monosemantic features that match LOCAL morphological annotations — cell types, calcifications, tissue classes. Concept-MIL and ConceptCLIP link patch concepts to diagnosis; representational-similarity and robustness-index papers characterize global embedding geometry and center confounds. Critically, all validate features against MARGINAL, local annotations (what a patch contains), not against the SPATIAL-RELATIONAL organization among cells/regions that constitutes the actual cancer-biology mechanism (immune infiltration, exclusion, boundary structure) known to drive prognosis. No prior work tests whether pathology FMs encode neighborhood-context relational structure, whether that structure is causally used, or whether it aligns with spatial-transcriptomics ground truth. Our relational, causal, spatial-omics-grounded test is the novel contribution, moving beyond 'the model recognizes cell X' to 'the model represents that immune cells are excluded from the tumor nest.'", 'Abstract': "Interpretability studies of pathology foundation models have shown they encode local morphological concepts such as cell types and tissue classes. But the mechanism that actually governs cancer behavior and prognosis is relational and spatial: whether cytotoxic lymphocytes infiltrate a tumor nest or are excluded from it, the geometry of the tumor-stroma boundary, and immune-desert versus inflamed ecologies. We ask whether pathology foundation models internally represent this spatial-relational tumor-microenvironment (TME) structure, beyond a bag of local morphologies, and whether that structure is causally load-bearing and biologically faithful. Using public whole-slide datasets with paired or co-registered spatial transcriptomics (e.g., 10x Xenium/Visium colorectal and lung cohorts) and immunohistochemistry, we extract patch- and neighborhood-level representations from several public pathology FMs (UNI2, Virchow2, Prov-GigaPath, CONCH). First, we build relational probes that predict TME relationships defined from cell segmentation and spatial statistics — infiltration score, immune-tumor colocalization/exclusion, boundary curvature — and test whether these are decodable beyond local cell-count baselines. Second, we run causal context-ablation experiments: we swap or scramble a patch's spatial neighbors and measure whether relational features and prognostic predictions change as predicted by TME biology (e.g., breaking infiltration should shift an immune-inflamed feature toward immune-desert). Third, we validate against spatial transcriptomics by testing whether relational features align with measured immune-signaling gene programs (T-cell cytotoxicity, IFN-gamma) at the same tissue location. Metrics quantify a relational-beyond-local index, context-ablation causal selectivity, and transcriptomic alignment. Either outcome sharpens what these clinically-deployed models actually 'see.'", 'Experiments': ["1. Data and ground-truth relations: Assemble public WSI cohorts with (a) cell segmentation/classification (via HoVer-Net or provided annotations) and (b) co-located spatial transcriptomics (10x Xenium/Visium colorectal, lung; or IHC for CD8/CD3/PanCK). Define ground-truth TME relational descriptors per neighborhood window: immune infiltration score (fraction of lymphocytes within tumor nests), immune-tumor spatial exclusion (cross-type Ripley's K / colocalization), tumor-stroma boundary curvature/interface length, and inflamed/excluded/desert phenotype label.", "2. Representation extraction: For UNI2, Virchow2, Prov-GigaPath, CONCH, extract patch embeddings and, using each model's spatial context (patch grids / slide-aggregator neighborhood attention where available), extract neighborhood-context representations. Train sparse autoencoders on these to obtain candidate relational features, in addition to raw-embedding linear probes.", "3. Relational-beyond-local test: Train probes predicting each relational descriptor from model representations. Compare against a strict LOCAL baseline that has access only to the same window's cell-type counts/densities (no spatial arrangement). Primary metric: Relational-Beyond-Local Index = probe R^2(model) minus probe R^2(local-count baseline). Positive index for infiltration/exclusion/boundary indicates encoded spatial relations beyond bag-of-cells.", '4. Neighborhood-permutation control: Spatially scramble cells within a window while preserving cell-type counts, re-render/re-embed (or, for grid models, permute neighbor patches). If model features are truly relational, predictions of infiltration/exclusion should collapse under scrambling while local-morphology features remain stable. Metric: relational-feature sensitivity to scrambling vs. local-feature stability.', "5. Causal context-ablation: For patch-context or neighborhood-attention models, replace a tumor patch's true immune-rich neighbors with immune-poor neighbors (and vice versa) and measure whether the recovered 'inflamed' relational feature and the downstream prognostic/subtype prediction shift in the biologically-correct direction. Controls: swap with neighbors matched on staining/center but of the same TME phenotype (should not shift). Metric: context-ablation causal selectivity = |shift_relation-breaking| / |shift_matched-control|.", '6. Spatial-transcriptomics faithfulness: At co-registered locations, test whether recovered relational features correlate with measured immune-signaling gene programs (T-cell cytotoxicity, IFN-gamma response, exhaustion) beyond what local cell-type composition predicts. Metric: partial Spearman correlation controlling for local cell composition; a mechanistic representation should retain signal after this control.', "7. Prognostic load-bearing test: In cohorts with survival/outcome, quantify how much prognostic performance is attributable to relational features vs. local features via feature-group ablation, testing whether the model's clinically-relevant signal rides on encoded TME relations.", '8. Cross-model and center-confound checks: Report which models encode relational structure most strongly, and control for medical-center/stain confounds (using the Robustness-Index approach) to ensure relational signal is not a center artifact.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["Grid-based FMs process patches independently, so 'context' must come from the slide-level aggregator; models with weak or absent spatial context may show no relational structure by construction — we report per-model and use neighborhood-attention aggregators where available.", 'Re-rendering scrambled tissue for causal ablation is nontrivial; for patch-grid models we operate at the neighbor-patch level (permute/swap embeddings), which is feasible, while pixel-level scrambling is used only where re-embedding is cheap.', 'Co-registration between H&E and spatial transcriptomics is imperfect, adding noise to the transcriptomic-faithfulness test; we use datasets with high-quality registration and report registration-error sensitivity.', 'Local cell counts and spatial relations are correlated in real tissue, making the relational-beyond-local separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with matched controls and partial-correlation analyses but residual confounding may attenuate effects.', 'Center/stain confounds strongly organize pathology embeddings (per the Robustness-Index literature); relational signal could be confounded and we explicitly control for it.', 'A negative result (models encode only local morphology, no relational TME structure) is scientifically decisive and clinically important, though possibly perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as a sharpening of what deployed pathology FMs represent.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public data, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

