Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "reading_frame_degeneracy_probe",
"Title": "Does the Model Read in Frame? Causally Testing Whether Genomic Foundation Models Internally Represent the Triplet Reading Frame and Codon-Degeneracy Structure of the Genetic Code",
"Short Hypothesis": "The genetic code is an exactly-known discrete mechanism: nucleotides are read in non-overlapping triplets anchored by a reading frame, and 61 sense codons map many-to-one onto 20 amino acids (degeneracy). We hypothesize that genomic foundation models trained on raw nucleotides internally represent this mechanism rather than mere k-mer statistics, meaning (a) their internal representations are frame-aware (encode a codon's position modulo 3 relative to the true reading frame), (b) synonymous codons are organized by their shared amino-acid identity (degeneracy geometry), and (c) this frame structure is causally load-bearing: a single-nucleotide frameshift insertion catastrophically and specifically reorganizes downstream codon-level representations and predictions, whereas a length-matched in-frame (3-nucleotide) insertion does not. Frameshift-vs-in-frame is an exact counterfactual matched on length and local composition, making this a rare mechanism-defining rather than correlation-matching test.",
"Related Work": "Interpretability of genomic FMs has surged: Interpreting Evo 2 (2025) and Decode-gLM (2025) use SAEs to recover coding-sequence, tRNA/rRNA, and viral features; SAE studies on small gene LMs and Nucleotide Transformer recover transcription-factor motifs; nucleotide-dependency analysis (2024) reveals functional elements; cdsFM (2024) reports models 'recapitulate the overall structure of the genetic code'; Genomic Tokenizer (2025) builds codon/frame biology into tokenization by construction. Crucially, all of these are descriptive annotation-matching or build codon structure in by design. cdsFM shows the map is learnable when codons are tokenized, but does not test nucleotide-level models, nor whether the reading FRAME (position mod 3, the anchoring mechanism) is internally represented and causally used, nor whether degeneracy (synonymous equivalence) is geometrically encoded independent of local composition. No prior work uses the exact frameshift-vs-in-frame counterfactual to causally arbitrate whether a nucleotide-level FM represents the triplet-reading mechanism versus k-mer statistics. This causal, exact-counterfactual, degeneracy-geometry test is our novel contribution.",
"Abstract": "The genetic code is one of biology's few exactly-known, discrete mechanisms: DNA is read in non-overlapping triplets anchored by a reading frame, and 61 sense codons map many-to-one onto 20 amino acids. This makes it an ideal, unambiguous probe for whether genomic foundation models learn mechanism or merely nucleotide-level statistics. We ask three mechanism-defining questions of nucleotide-level genomic FMs (Nucleotide Transformer, DNABERT-2, and Evo 2). First, are internal representations frame-aware: can we linearly decode a nucleotide's position modulo 3 relative to the true reading frame, beyond what local composition permits, and is a distinct feature dedicated to frame? Second, is codon degeneracy geometrically encoded: do synonymous codons cluster by their shared amino acid while remaining separable at the codon level, controlling for GC-content and dinucleotide statistics? Third, and most decisively, is frame structure causally load-bearing? We construct an exact counterfactual: inserting a single nucleotide (a frameshift) versus inserting a length-matched triplet (in-frame) within a coding sequence. A model that represents the reading mechanism should show catastrophic, specific downstream reorganization of codon-level representations and amino-acid predictions after a frameshift but near-invariance after an in-frame insertion; a k-mer statistical model should respond similarly to both. We further steer the recovered frame feature and test whether it induces frameshift-like downstream effects. Metrics quantify a frame-decodability-beyond-composition index, a degeneracy-clustering score, and a frameshift-vs-in-frame causal asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens what genomic foundation models actually represent, with direct implications for variant-effect and indel interpretation.",
"Experiments": [
"1. Models and data: Load public nucleotide-level genomic FMs (Nucleotide Transformer, DNABERT-2, Evo 2 at a feasible size). Curate a set of well-annotated coding sequences (CDS) from RefSeq/Ensembl across several species with known reading frames, start/stop codons, and codon-level positions. Extract per-position/per-token hidden activations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.",
"2. Frame-awareness probe: Train linear probes to predict a nucleotide's position modulo 3 (relative to the annotated reading frame) from its representation, per layer. Report accuracy. Strict baselines: (a) probe raw one-hot/local-k-mer features (to show frame signal exceeds local composition), and (b) probe a randomly-initialized model of the same architecture (to confirm frame structure is learned during pretraining, following Decode-gLM's control). Primary metric: Frame-Decodability-Beyond-Composition Index = probe accuracy(model) minus probe accuracy(local-composition baseline).",
"3. Degeneracy geometry: For each codon occurrence, extract its codon-level representation (mean-pool the triplet or use the codon-token embedding). Test whether synonymous codons cluster by amino acid via (i) silhouette/clustering score of codons colored by amino acid, and (ii) a nested classification test: amino-acid decodable AND codon-identity still separable within amino acid. Control for GC content and dinucleotide frequency by regressing them out and re-measuring clustering (partial-clustering score). Compare degeneracy structure across the model's codon table blocks (e.g., 4-fold, 2-fold, 6-fold degenerate boxes) to see if learned geometry mirrors the true code's degeneracy pattern.",
"4. Frameshift-vs-in-frame causal counterfactual (primary test): Within each CDS, at matched interior positions, construct paired edits: (a) insert 1 nucleotide (frameshift) and (b) insert 3 nucleotides (in-frame), with insertions matched on local nucleotide composition. Measure the change in downstream codon-level representations and in the model's per-position amino-acid / codon predictions (or masked-token predictions) at positions after the insertion. Metric: Frameshift-vs-In-frame Causal Asymmetry = downstream-representation-change(frameshift) / downstream-representation-change(in-frame). Mechanistic model => asymmetry >> 1 (frameshift catastrophically reorganizes downstream codons); k-mer model => asymmetry ~ 1. Report as a function of distance downstream of the insertion.",
"5. Amino-acid consistency of frameshift effects: For coding-aware readouts (translate model-preferred codons, or use Evo 2's coding-sequence feature), test whether the frameshifted downstream reading yields the shifted-frame amino-acid sequence (the biologically-correct consequence) rather than random reorganization. Metric: agreement between induced downstream codon reinterpretation and the ground-truth +1/+2 shifted reading frame.",
"6. Causal frame-feature steering: Using the recovered frame direction/SAE frame feature, add matched-magnitude perturbations to shift the encoded frame at a chosen position and test whether downstream representations/predictions move toward the frameshift-predicted state (as in Experiment 4). Controls: random matched-norm directions and shuffled-frame-label directions should not induce frameshift-like effects. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect_frame_direction| / |effect_random_direction|.",
"7. Stop-codon / start-codon anchoring: Test whether representations encode start/stop as frame anchors: does inserting a premature in-frame stop codon reorganize downstream coding representations (loss-of-coding signal) more than a synonymous same-length non-stop change? This probes whether frame is anchored by biologically-meaningful signals, not arbitrary position.",
"8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where frame-awareness and degeneracy geometry emerge across depth, and whether the recovered frame subspaces align across the three models (CKA), assessing whether the reading-frame mechanism is a convergent internal structure.",
"9. Brackets: Compare against (upper bound) a codon-tokenized model such as cdsFM/EnCodon where frame is built in, and (lower bound) a model trained on shuffled/frame-scrambled sequences, to place nucleotide-level FMs on the statistics-to-mechanism spectrum."
],
"Risk Factors and Limitations": [
"Frame position modulo 3 correlates with local codon composition in real CDS, so frame decodability could partly reflect composition; we mitigate with strict local-composition baselines, GC/dinucleotide controls, and the exact frameshift-vs-in-frame counterfactual which is matched on length and local composition.",
"Some models (e.g., BPE-tokenized DNABERT-2) do not tokenize per-nucleotide or per-codon, complicating position-mod-3 alignment; we handle this by mapping token spans to nucleotide positions and report per-model tokenization caveats, prioritizing single-nucleotide models (Evo 2) for the cleanest test.",
"Steering the frame feature in a nonlinear model may push activations off-manifold and yield uninterpretable outputs; we bound perturbation magnitude, report sensitivity, and use random/shuffled-label controls, relying primarily on the input-space frameshift counterfactual as causal evidence.",
"Distributed/superposed encodings may mean no single clean 'frame' feature exists; we use both linear probe directions and SAE features and report aggregate-direction results.",
"A negative result (models respond similarly to frameshift and in-frame insertions, i.e., k-mer statistics dominate) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting these models for indel/variant-effect prediction, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first exact-mechanism causal test of the reading frame.",
"All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public genome annotations, keeping compute at academic scale."
]
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'reading_frame_degeneracy_probe', 'Title': 'Does the Model Read in Frame? Causally Testing Whether Genomic Foundation Models Internally Represent the Triplet Reading Frame and Codon-Degeneracy Structure of the Genetic Code', 'Short Hypothesis': "The genetic code is an exactly-known discrete mechanism: nucleotides are read in non-overlapping triplets anchored by a reading frame, and 61 sense codons map many-to-one onto 20 amino acids (degeneracy). We hypothesize that genomic foundation models trained on raw nucleotides internally represent this mechanism rather than mere k-mer statistics, meaning (a) their internal representations are frame-aware (encode a codon's position modulo 3 relative to the true reading frame), (b) synonymous codons are organized by their shared amino-acid identity (degeneracy geometry), and (c) this frame structure is causally load-bearing: a single-nucleotide frameshift insertion catastrophically and specifically reorganizes downstream codon-level representations and predictions, whereas a length-matched in-frame (3-nucleotide) insertion does not. Frameshift-vs-in-frame is an exact counterfactual matched on length and local composition, making this a rare mechanism-defining rather than correlation-matching test.", 'Related Work': "Interpretability of genomic FMs has surged: Interpreting Evo 2 (2025) and Decode-gLM (2025) use SAEs to recover coding-sequence, tRNA/rRNA, and viral features; SAE studies on small gene LMs and Nucleotide Transformer recover transcription-factor motifs; nucleotide-dependency analysis (2024) reveals functional elements; cdsFM (2024) reports models 'recapitulate the overall structure of the genetic code'; Genomic Tokenizer (2025) builds codon/frame biology into tokenization by construction. Crucially, all of these are descriptive annotation-matching or build codon structure in by design. cdsFM shows the map is learnable when codons are tokenized, but does not test nucleotide-level models, nor whether the reading FRAME (position mod 3, the anchoring mechanism) is internally represented and causally used, nor whether degeneracy (synonymous equivalence) is geometrically encoded independent of local composition. No prior work uses the exact frameshift-vs-in-frame counterfactual to causally arbitrate whether a nucleotide-level FM represents the triplet-reading mechanism versus k-mer statistics. This causal, exact-counterfactual, degeneracy-geometry test is our novel contribution.", 'Abstract': "The genetic code is one of biology's few exactly-known, discrete mechanisms: DNA is read in non-overlapping triplets anchored by a reading frame, and 61 sense codons map many-to-one onto 20 amino acids. This makes it an ideal, unambiguous probe for whether genomic foundation models learn mechanism or merely nucleotide-level statistics. We ask three mechanism-defining questions of nucleotide-level genomic FMs (Nucleotide Transformer, DNABERT-2, and Evo 2). First, are internal representations frame-aware: can we linearly decode a nucleotide's position modulo 3 relative to the true reading frame, beyond what local composition permits, and is a distinct feature dedicated to frame? Second, is codon degeneracy geometrically encoded: do synonymous codons cluster by their shared amino acid while remaining separable at the codon level, controlling for GC-content and dinucleotide statistics? Third, and most decisively, is frame structure causally load-bearing? We construct an exact counterfactual: inserting a single nucleotide (a frameshift) versus inserting a length-matched triplet (in-frame) within a coding sequence. A model that represents the reading mechanism should show catastrophic, specific downstream reorganization of codon-level representations and amino-acid predictions after a frameshift but near-invariance after an in-frame insertion; a k-mer statistical model should respond similarly to both. We further steer the recovered frame feature and test whether it induces frameshift-like downstream effects. Metrics quantify a frame-decodability-beyond-composition index, a degeneracy-clustering score, and a frameshift-vs-in-frame causal asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens what genomic foundation models actually represent, with direct implications for variant-effect and indel interpretation.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load public nucleotide-level genomic FMs (Nucleotide Transformer, DNABERT-2, Evo 2 at a feasible size). Curate a set of well-annotated coding sequences (CDS) from RefSeq/Ensembl across several species with known reading frames, start/stop codons, and codon-level positions. Extract per-position/per-token hidden activations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', "2. Frame-awareness probe: Train linear probes to predict a nucleotide's position modulo 3 (relative to the annotated reading frame) from its representation, per layer. Report accuracy. Strict baselines: (a) probe raw one-hot/local-k-mer features (to show frame signal exceeds local composition), and (b) probe a randomly-initialized model of the same architecture (to confirm frame structure is learned during pretraining, following Decode-gLM's control). Primary metric: Frame-Decodability-Beyond-Composition Index = probe accuracy(model) minus probe accuracy(local-composition baseline).", "3. Degeneracy geometry: For each codon occurrence, extract its codon-level representation (mean-pool the triplet or use the codon-token embedding). Test whether synonymous codons cluster by amino acid via (i) silhouette/clustering score of codons colored by amino acid, and (ii) a nested classification test: amino-acid decodable AND codon-identity still separable within amino acid. Control for GC content and dinucleotide frequency by regressing them out and re-measuring clustering (partial-clustering score). Compare degeneracy structure across the model's codon table blocks (e.g., 4-fold, 2-fold, 6-fold degenerate boxes) to see if learned geometry mirrors the true code's degeneracy pattern.", "4. Frameshift-vs-in-frame causal counterfactual (primary test): Within each CDS, at matched interior positions, construct paired edits: (a) insert 1 nucleotide (frameshift) and (b) insert 3 nucleotides (in-frame), with insertions matched on local nucleotide composition. Measure the change in downstream codon-level representations and in the model's per-position amino-acid / codon predictions (or masked-token predictions) at positions after the insertion. Metric: Frameshift-vs-In-frame Causal Asymmetry = downstream-representation-change(frameshift) / downstream-representation-change(in-frame). Mechanistic model => asymmetry >> 1 (frameshift catastrophically reorganizes downstream codons); k-mer model => asymmetry ~ 1. Report as a function of distance downstream of the insertion.", "5. Amino-acid consistency of frameshift effects: For coding-aware readouts (translate model-preferred codons, or use Evo 2's coding-sequence feature), test whether the frameshifted downstream reading yields the shifted-frame amino-acid sequence (the biologically-correct consequence) rather than random reorganization. Metric: agreement between induced downstream codon reinterpretation and the ground-truth +1/+2 shifted reading frame.", '6. Causal frame-feature steering: Using the recovered frame direction/SAE frame feature, add matched-magnitude perturbations to shift the encoded frame at a chosen position and test whether downstream representations/predictions move toward the frameshift-predicted state (as in Experiment 4). Controls: random matched-norm directions and shuffled-frame-label directions should not induce frameshift-like effects. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect_frame_direction| / |effect_random_direction|.', '7. Stop-codon / start-codon anchoring: Test whether representations encode start/stop as frame anchors: does inserting a premature in-frame stop codon reorganize downstream coding representations (loss-of-coding signal) more than a synonymous same-length non-stop change? This probes whether frame is anchored by biologically-meaningful signals, not arbitrary position.', '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where frame-awareness and degeneracy geometry emerge across depth, and whether the recovered frame subspaces align across the three models (CKA), assessing whether the reading-frame mechanism is a convergent internal structure.', '9. Brackets: Compare against (upper bound) a codon-tokenized model such as cdsFM/EnCodon where frame is built in, and (lower bound) a model trained on shuffled/frame-scrambled sequences, to place nucleotide-level FMs on the statistics-to-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Frame position modulo 3 correlates with local codon composition in real CDS, so frame decodability could partly reflect composition; we mitigate with strict local-composition baselines, GC/dinucleotide controls, and the exact frameshift-vs-in-frame counterfactual which is matched on length and local composition.', 'Some models (e.g., BPE-tokenized DNABERT-2) do not tokenize per-nucleotide or per-codon, complicating position-mod-3 alignment; we handle this by mapping token spans to nucleotide positions and report per-model tokenization caveats, prioritizing single-nucleotide models (Evo 2) for the cleanest test.', 'Steering the frame feature in a nonlinear model may push activations off-manifold and yield uninterpretable outputs; we bound perturbation magnitude, report sensitivity, and use random/shuffled-label controls, relying primarily on the input-space frameshift counterfactual as causal evidence.', "Distributed/superposed encodings may mean no single clean 'frame' feature exists; we use both linear probe directions and SAE features and report aggregate-direction results.", 'A negative result (models respond similarly to frameshift and in-frame insertions, i.e., k-mer statistics dominate) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting these models for indel/variant-effect prediction, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first exact-mechanism causal test of the reading frame.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public genome annotations, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

