Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "saha_boltzmann_abundance_probe",
  "Title": "Does the Model Know the Curve of Growth? Testing Whether Stellar Foundation Models Disentangle Abundance from Temperature via Saha-Boltzmann Line Physics",
  "Short Hypothesis": "The strength of an absorption line in a stellar spectrum is not a monotone readout of elemental abundance: it is governed by the Saha-Boltzmann excitation/ionization equilibrium (line strength depends on temperature through level populations) and by the nonlinear curve of growth (linear -> saturated -> damping regimes). We hypothesize that if a stellar foundation model represents this mechanism rather than surface line-depth statistics, its internal abundance representation will (a) be invariant to temperature-driven line-strength changes at fixed abundance, (b) follow the curve of growth (saturating with respect to equivalent width) rather than tracking raw line depth, and (c) encode the physically-mandated coupling among multiple lines of the same element that share excitation potential. An exact counterfactual - vary Teff at fixed [Fe/H] versus vary [Fe/H] at fixed Teff - makes this a mechanism-defining rather than correlation-matching test.",
  "Related Work": "Astronomy interpretability has grown quickly: SAEs on galaxy morphology (Euclid Q1, 2025; SFNet, 2024), attention analyses showing SpectraFM (2024) attends to chemically-informative wavelengths, disentangled chemical-tagging networks (Price-Jones/Bovy line, 2021), symbolic regression from GNNs for cosmology (2020), and interpretable anomaly/attribution methods for spectra (2021-2025). AstroCLIP (2024) and OJALA (2026) provide cross-modal / SED foundation models that decode stellar parameters. All of these either (i) attribute which wavelengths matter, (ii) recover monosemantic morphology features, or (iii) show a parameter is decodable. None tests whether a stellar foundation model internally represents the Saha-Boltzmann/curve-of-growth mechanism that determines how abundance maps to line strength, whether the model correctly disentangles the well-known abundance-Teff degeneracy at the representational level, or whether its internal abundance direction is causally load-bearing and physics-consistent. Because line strength is jointly set by abundance and temperature via an exactly-known physical law, this is a rare setting where mechanism can be cleanly separated from correlation. Our counterfactual, curve-of-growth, and multi-line-coupling tests are the novel contribution.",
  "Abstract": "Stellar spectra are among the most physically constrained data in science: the strength of any absorption line is set jointly by elemental abundance and by the Saha-Boltzmann excitation/ionization equilibrium through temperature-dependent level populations, and it grows with abundance along a nonlinear curve of growth (linear, saturated, and damping regimes). This makes stellar foundation models an ideal testbed for whether scientific foundation models learn mechanism or surface statistics. We ask whether stellar spectral foundation models (e.g., SpectraFM, AstroCLIP's spectrum encoder, and open transformer spectral models) internally represent this line-formation mechanism. First, we test disentanglement of the classic abundance-temperature degeneracy: using synthetic spectra where we can hold [Fe/H] fixed while varying Teff (which strongly changes Fe line strengths) and vice versa, we probe whether the model's internal 'abundance' direction is invariant to Teff-driven line-strength changes at fixed abundance and responds selectively to true abundance changes. Second, we test curve-of-growth structure: does the internal abundance representation saturate (as physics dictates) rather than track raw line depth or equivalent width linearly? Third, we test multi-line coupling: lines of the same element sharing excitation potential must vary together per Saha-Boltzmann; we test whether the model encodes this coupling and whether it correctly separates high- versus low-excitation lines' temperature sensitivity. We validate causally by ablating/steering the recovered abundance direction and by transferring from synthetic to real spectra (APOGEE/GALAH). Metrics quantify a temperature-invariance-of-abundance index, curve-of-growth agreement, and multi-line coupling fidelity. Either outcome sharpens what stellar foundation models actually represent, with implications for trusting them in low-metallicity and parameter-degenerate regimes.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Models and data: Load public stellar spectral foundation models (SpectraFM; AstroCLIP spectrum encoder; and an open transformer spectrum model if available). Generate a grid of synthetic stellar spectra with a standard radiative-transfer code (e.g., Turbospectrum/MOOG with MARLES/MARCS atmospheres) spanning Teff, log g, and [Fe/H] (plus a few individual element abundances), where the ground-truth abundance and temperature are exactly known and can be varied independently. Also assemble real labeled spectra from APOGEE and GALAH for transfer. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.",
    "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes on internal activations (per layer) to decode Teff, log g, and [Fe/H], confirming each is encoded and choosing best layers (replicating prior decodability findings as a control).",
    "3. Abundance-temperature disentanglement (primary counterfactual): Construct matched pairs - (a) fix [Fe/H], vary Teff over a range that produces large Fe line-strength changes; (b) fix Teff, vary [Fe/H] by a matched line-strength amount. Measure the change in the model's internal decoded-abundance direction for each. Primary metric: Temperature-Invariance-of-Abundance Index = |delta_abundance_representation under (b)| / |delta_abundance_representation under (a)|. A mechanistic model gives index >> 1 (abundance representation moves with true abundance, not with Teff-driven line strength); a line-depth statistic gives index ~ 1.",
    "4. Curve-of-growth test: For a fixed line/element, sweep abundance across linear, saturated, and damping regimes (computing the true equivalent width curve of growth from the synthesis). Test whether the model's internal abundance readout follows the saturating curve-of-growth relationship versus tracking raw line depth or equivalent width linearly. Metric: correlation/goodness-of-fit of internal abundance vs. true log-abundance across regimes, and whether the internal representation reproduces the saturation plateau. Baseline: a raw-equivalent-width readout (which saturates) vs. raw line-depth readout (which does not).",
    "5. Multi-line Saha-Boltzmann coupling: For an element with multiple lines of differing excitation potential (e.g., Fe I lines), Saha-Boltzmann dictates that low- and high-excitation lines have different Teff sensitivity but a shared abundance dependence. Test whether the model (a) encodes a shared abundance factor across these lines and (b) correctly assigns stronger Teff sensitivity to the physically-appropriate lines. Metric: alignment between per-line temperature-sensitivity in the representation (measured by masking/occluding each line region) and the Saha-Boltzmann-predicted sensitivity; and a shared-abundance-subspace coherence score across lines of the same element vs. different elements (control).",
    "6. Causal ablation/steering: Ablate the recovered abundance direction and measure degradation of abundance prediction while Teff prediction is preserved (and vice versa), testing that the disentangled directions are causally load-bearing and not entangled. Steer along the abundance direction and check that reconstructed/predicted spectra (where a decoder or masked head exists) change in the curve-of-growth-correct direction. Controls: matched-norm random directions.",
    "7. Synthetic-to-real transfer: Test whether the disentanglement and curve-of-growth structure recovered on synthetic spectra hold on real APOGEE/GALAH spectra, using their pipeline labels as ground truth, assessing robustness of the mechanism beyond idealized synthesis.",
    "8. Low-metallicity / degeneracy stress test: Evaluate whether disentanglement degrades in regimes where lines are weak (metal-poor stars) or where abundance-Teff degeneracy is strongest, and whether the temperature-invariance index predicts where the model's abundance estimates become unreliable (AUROC for flagging high-error stars).",
    "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where disentangled abundance structure emerges across depth and whether the abundance subspaces align across models (CKA), assessing whether Saha-Boltzmann-consistent structure is a convergent internal property."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "Abundance and Teff are correlated in real stellar populations, so disentanglement could be confounded; we mitigate with synthetic spectra where the two are varied exactly independently and use matched-line-strength controls.",
    "Synthetic spectra may differ from real spectra (synthetic gap), so representations learned on real data may not fully engage the synthetic grid; we test synthetic-to-real transfer explicitly and, where possible, use models pre-trained partly on synthetic data (e.g., SpectraFM).",
    "Linear probes recovering an abundance direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with ablation/steering and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.",
    "Some models tokenize/normalize spectra (continuum normalization, resampling) in ways that may alter line-strength information; we document each model's preprocessing and probe pre-normalization activations where feasible.",
    "Occlusion-based per-line sensitivity measurements can be noisy for blended lines; we restrict multi-line coupling tests to clean, well-separated lines identified from the synthesis.",
    "A negative result (models track line-strength statistics and do not disentangle abundance from temperature per Saha-Boltzmann) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting these models in degenerate/metal-poor regimes, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of line-formation physics in stellar foundation models.",
    "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and standard synthesis tools/public spectral surveys, keeping compute at academic scale."
  ]
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'saha_boltzmann_abundance_probe', 'Title': 'Does the Model Know the Curve of Growth? Testing Whether Stellar Foundation Models Disentangle Abundance from Temperature via Saha-Boltzmann Line Physics', 'Short Hypothesis': 'The strength of an absorption line in a stellar spectrum is not a monotone readout of elemental abundance: it is governed by the Saha-Boltzmann excitation/ionization equilibrium (line strength depends on temperature through level populations) and by the nonlinear curve of growth (linear -> saturated -> damping regimes). We hypothesize that if a stellar foundation model represents this mechanism rather than surface line-depth statistics, its internal abundance representation will (a) be invariant to temperature-driven line-strength changes at fixed abundance, (b) follow the curve of growth (saturating with respect to equivalent width) rather than tracking raw line depth, and (c) encode the physically-mandated coupling among multiple lines of the same element that share excitation potential. An exact counterfactual - vary Teff at fixed [Fe/H] versus vary [Fe/H] at fixed Teff - makes this a mechanism-defining rather than correlation-matching test.', 'Related Work': 'Astronomy interpretability has grown quickly: SAEs on galaxy morphology (Euclid Q1, 2025; SFNet, 2024), attention analyses showing SpectraFM (2024) attends to chemically-informative wavelengths, disentangled chemical-tagging networks (Price-Jones/Bovy line, 2021), symbolic regression from GNNs for cosmology (2020), and interpretable anomaly/attribution methods for spectra (2021-2025). AstroCLIP (2024) and OJALA (2026) provide cross-modal / SED foundation models that decode stellar parameters. All of these either (i) attribute which wavelengths matter, (ii) recover monosemantic morphology features, or (iii) show a parameter is decodable. None tests whether a stellar foundation model internally represents the Saha-Boltzmann/curve-of-growth mechanism that determine