eparates high- versus low-excitation lines' temperature sensitivity. We validate causally by ablating/steering the recovered abundance direction and by transferring from synthetic to real spectra (APOGEE/GALAH). Metrics quantify a temperature-invariance-of-abundance index, curve-of-growth agreement, and multi-line coupling fidelity. Either outcome sharpens what stellar foundation models actually represent, with implications for trusting them in low-metallicity and parameter-degenerate regimes.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load public stellar spectral foundation models (SpectraFM; AstroCLIP spectrum encoder; and an open transformer spectrum model if available). Generate a grid of synthetic stellar spectra with a standard radiative-transfer code (e.g., Turbospectrum/MOOG with MARLES/MARCS atmospheres) spanning Teff, log g, and [Fe/H] (plus a few individual element abundances), where the ground-truth abundance and temperature are exactly known and can be varied independently. Also assemble real labeled spectra from APOGEE and GALAH for transfer. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes on internal activations (per layer) to decode Teff, log g, and [Fe/H], confirming each is encoded and choosing best layers (replicating prior decodability findings as a control).', "3. Abundance-temperature disentanglement (primary counterfactual): Construct matched pairs - (a) fix [Fe/H], vary Teff over a range that produces large Fe line-strength changes; (b) fix Teff, vary [Fe/H] by a matched line-strength amount. Measure the change in the model's internal decoded-abundance direction for each. Primary metric: Temperature-Invariance-of-Abundance Index = |delta_abundance_representation under (b)| / |delta_abundance_representation under (a)|. A mechanistic model gives index >> 1 (abundance representation moves with true abundance, not with Teff-driven line strength); a line-depth statistic gives index ~ 1.", "4. Curve-of-growth test: For a fixed line/element, sweep abundance across linear, saturated, and damping regimes (computing the true equivalent width curve of growth from the synthesis). Test whether the model's internal abundance readout follows the saturating curve-of-growth relationship versus tracking raw line depth or equivalent width linearly. Metric: correlation/goodness-of-fit of internal abundance vs. true log-abundance across regimes, and whether the internal representation reproduces the saturation plateau. Baseline: a raw-equivalent-width readout (which saturates) vs. raw line-depth readout (which does not).", '5. Multi-line Saha-Boltzmann coupling: For an element with multiple lines of differing excitation potential (e.g., Fe I lines), Saha-Boltzmann dictates that low- and high-excitation lines have different Teff sensitivity but a shared abundance dependence. Test whether the model (a) encodes a shared abundance factor across these lines and (b) correctly assigns stronger Teff sensitivity to the physically-appropriate lines. Metric: alignment between per-line temperature-sensitivity in the representation (measured by masking/occluding each line region) and the Saha-Boltzmann-predicted sensitivity; and a shared-abundance-subspace coherence score across lines of the same element vs. different elements (control).', '6. Causal ablation/steering: Ablate the recovered abundance direction and measure degradation of abundance prediction while Teff prediction is preserved (and vice versa), testing that the disentangled directions are causally load-bearing and not entangled. Steer along the abundance direction and check that reconstructed/predicted spectra (where a decoder or masked head exists) change in the curve-of-growth-correct direction. Controls: matched-norm random directions.', '7. Synthetic-to-real transfer: Test whether the disentanglement and curve-of-growth structure recovered on synthetic spectra hold on real APOGEE/GALAH spectra, using their pipeline labels as ground truth, assessing robustness of the mechanism beyond idealized synthesis.', "8. Low-metallicity / degeneracy stress test: Evaluate whether disentanglement degrades in regimes where lines are weak (metal-poor stars) or where abundance-Teff degeneracy is strongest, and whether the temperature-invariance index predicts where the model's abundance estimates become unreliable (AUROC for flagging high-error stars).", '9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where disentangled abundance structure emerges across depth and whether the abundance subspaces align across models (CKA), assessing whether Saha-Boltzmann-consistent structure is a convergent internal property.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Abundance and Teff are correlated in real stellar populations, so disentanglement could be confounded; we mitigate with synthetic spectra where the two are varied exactly independently and use matched-line-strength controls.', 'Synthetic spectra may differ from real spectra (synthetic gap), so representations learned on real data may not fully engage the synthetic grid; we test synthetic-to-real transfer explicitly and, where possible, use models pre-trained partly on synthetic data (e.g., SpectraFM).', 'Linear probes recovering an abundance direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with ablation/steering and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', "Some models tokenize/normalize spectra (continuum normalization, resampling) in ways that may alter line-strength information; we document each model's preprocessing and probe pre-normalization activations where feasible.", 'Occlusion-based per-line sensitivity measurements can be noisy for blended lines; we restrict multi-line coupling tests to clean, well-separated lines identified from the synthesis.', 'A negative result (models track line-strength statistics and do not disentangle abundance from temperature p